## How to Plan

**Step 1 — Choose 2–3 batch components** that will be cooked once and used across the whole week:
- One protein (e.g. roasted chicken thighs, ground beef, baked salmon, lentils)
- One grain/starch (e.g. brown rice, quinoa, roasted potatoes, farro)
- One vegetable (e.g. roasted mixed veg, sautéed greens, roasted sweet potato)

{% if ingredient_overlap == "high" %}
Use the **same 2–3 components in every single meal** — shortest shopping list, maximum efficiency.
{% elif ingredient_overlap == "low" %}
You may introduce a second protein or grain mid-week for variety, but keep it to one addition.
{% else %}
Stick to the same 2–3 components throughout. Vary the experience with sauces, toppings, and fresh additions.
{% endif %}

**Step 2 — Design every meal slot as an assembly:**
- `batch_component`: which batch component(s) this meal uses (e.g. "roasted chicken thighs, brown rice")
- `meal_concept`: name the finished assembled dish (e.g. "Sesame Chicken Bowl with Cucumber and Rice", "Chicken Hummus Wrap with Pickled Onion")
- `estimated_prep_min`: 5–10 (assembly only)
- `estimated_cook_min`: 0–3 (warming only)

**Step 3 — Vary through toppings and sauces, not new cooking:**
- Sauces: teriyaki, tzatziki, tahini, pesto, hot sauce, soy-ginger
- Fresh toppings: avocado, herbs, pickled veg, nuts, cheese, a fried egg
- Do NOT introduce new proteins or grains that require cooking

**Nutrition:** Ensure the batch components together cover protein, complex carbs, and vegetables.

